+import type { Finding } from "../finding.page-type.ts"
+
+export const aMessageGivenToWriteIsDroppedAndTheCommitCarriesAPlausibleSubstitute = {
+  id: "01a0785d-8ccd-7626-87d6-f7cfb18fc2a0",
+  pageTypeSlug: "finding",
+  slug: "a-message-given-to-write-is-dropped-and-the-commit-carries-a-plausible-substitute",
+  domainSlug: "workspace-package/command-system",
+  claim:
+    "`akasha write` declares `--message <text>` as `what the commit is for` and discards it. A write always drafts into a patch and makes no commit, so the message reaches `landing` and stops there, and the later `akasha patch apply`, given no message of its own, commits `the patch this agent drafted`. The loss is silent at both ends: the write answers `drafted` with no refusal and no mention of the flag, and the commit body reads as an ordinary default rather than as a gap. An empty message would be noticed; a plausible one is not. 313 of the 5012 patches applied over seven days carry that body, and no reader of any of them can tell which lost a reason.",
+  evidence:
+    "Read and run live at the akasha root on 2026-09-06.\n\nThe two declarations sit on one page. `command-system/commands/write/write.command.ts:16` states that `--message <text>` takes `what the commit is for`. Line 30 of the same file states that the change is kept in the patch beside this agent's page, and that `akasha patch apply` lands it. A write never reaches a commit for the message to be for.\n\nWhere the message stops. `write.command.code.ts:458` hands `message: built.message` to `landingAsked` beside `draft: true` at :463, which `writing` sets for every call. `asking.module.code.ts:393` works the message out, :395 routes to `draftingAsked` where `held.draft === true`, and :344 hands the message on to `landing`. `landing.module.code.ts:358` takes it as a parameter. At :389-400 the drafting branch returns `draftedBy(...)`, which is handed no message; :430, `committed(root, put.wrote, put.took, message, writer)`, is the only consumer of that parameter, and the drafting branch never reaches it. The patch file's own commit carries the fixed constant at `drafting.module.code.ts:34`, `is drafted into; the change it draws is not landed`, committed at :289 and :334.\n\nThe probe. `akasha write --file-path scratch/message-probe2.txt --message 'WRITE-SIDE-MESSAGE-PROBE-9931 given to write, not to apply'` drafted, and the `akasha patch apply` that followed carried no message of its own and committed e62426c5702cb337900e4c609734e35e8569312d, whose body is `the patch this agent drafted`. The message given to the write is nowhere in it. A second write, `--remove scratch/message-probe2.txt --message IGNORED-ON-WRITE-PROBE`, answered whole in three lines: `drafted`, then `14 checks judged the 1 path the patch would leave, and none refused`, then where the patch is kept. None of the three names the flag, and none refuses it.\n\nThe control that places the loss on the write road rather than anywhere later. `akasha patch apply --message 'PROBE-DISTINCTIVE-MESSAGE-4712 does patch apply keep this'` committed 48d71e6103da4f67790ec667b83cbd14190b2623 with exactly that body. A message handed to the apply is kept. Both probes named `--break-the-glass`, a file no page claims being refused by the checks, and the scratch file was taken away after.\n\nThe count and its shape. Over the last seven days git holds 5012 commits reading `the patch it held is applied` and 313 reading exactly `the patch this agent drafted`. Bucketed by the hour those 313 run steady from 09-03 to 09-06, between 2 and 25 an hour with no step in them, so this is how the road has behaved all along rather than a regression with a date.\n\nWhat I did not establish. Among the 313 I did not separate the agents that gave a message to the write from the agents that gave none at all. The commit body is the same either way, which is the point of filing it. a12baa54c2646c8cd356e006b8e6a538db9d2210 is the commit that prompted the look, and git does not say which of the two it was.",
+} as const satisfies Finding
